{
 "schemaVersion": 1,
 "id": "kukhnya-s01e02-scene-04",
 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Где мой нож?  (Where Is My Knife?)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "nastya": "Настя"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Chef Barinov has lost his favourite knife, and he is not going to let anyone forget it. Nastya is one of the people he questions. Here's the whole scene in Russian first, and don't worry about catching every word.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А где мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Кто взял мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, я не брала.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Повторяю. Кто взял мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я не знаю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Никто отсюда домой не уйдёт.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Пока вы не найдёте нож.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я не буду ничего готовить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Так, все ищем нож.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Живо!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "А что за проблема?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Мы такой же нож купим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А ты знаешь, что это за нож?",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go back and watch the chef lose his temper.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А где мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And where is my knife?' Literally, \"and where my knife\", with no \"is\" anywhere, since Russian drops the existence word for now. The little word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " in front just nudges the question along, like a weary \"so\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Кто взял мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Who took my knife?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Кто взял",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"who took\". The term for \"who\" always takes the default past form for a man, so it ends with a plain -l, whoever the thief turns out to be.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Шеф, я не брала.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, I didn't take it.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "я не брала.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means \"I not took\". Nastya switches to the everyday action word for taking, because she is denying any taking at all. The little \"a\" at the end shows she is a woman.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Повторяю. Кто взял мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I repeat: who took my knife?' The term for \"I repeat\" is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Повторяю.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", built from the term for a repeat. This is what you say when your patience has run out.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я не знаю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I don't know, chef.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я не знаю,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"I not know\", and is one of the most useful sentences in Russian. The little word for \"not\" goes right in front of the action word it cancels.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Никто отсюда домой не уйдёт.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Nobody will go home from here.' Russian doubles the negative, so \"nobody\" and \"not\" both appear before the action word. Going home is the single word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "домой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "отсюда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"from here\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Пока вы не найдёте нож.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Until you find the knife.' Russian has no separate word for \"until\" here. It translates to \"while you don't find\", so it uses ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Пока",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " plus the term for \"not\". And that first word is also the casual \"bye\", which really means \"for now\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Understood, chef.' The term for \"understood\" is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Понятно,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which literally means \"it's clear\". Nastya needs no \"I\" in it, and it's the usual way to show you got the message.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я не буду ничего готовить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will not cook anything.' The words for \"I will not\" and \"nothing\" both say no, and Russian insists on both. The action word for cooking is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "готовить.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", built from the describing word for \"ready\": cooking is getting things ready.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Так, все ищем нож.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Right, everyone, look for the knife.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Так,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " works as \"right\" or \"so\". ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "все ищем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"all of us look\", with the \"we\" form of the action word, so the chef counts himself in.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Живо!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Quick!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Живо!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is built from the describing word for \"alive\", so it translates to \"look lively\" or \"get a move on\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "А что за проблема?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is the problem?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "что за",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"what kind of\", so Nastya's question literally means \"what kind of problem?\". You can ask this about nearly anything.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Мы такой же нож купим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We will buy the same knife.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "такой же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"the same kind of\", and the term for \"we'll buy\" is a single action word. Nastya makes it sound very simple.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А ты знаешь, что это за нож?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And do you know what kind of knife this is?' Look for the phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "что это за",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " again: it translates to \"what kind of knife is this\". The chef uses the term for \"you\" in the friendly way to Nastya, as bosses do in kitchens. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А где мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Кто взял мой нож?",
   "gap": 0.5
